# YOU MAY NOT NEED THIS FILE. Run any command with a terminal attached -
#   python -m agent --tui
# - and if no key is configured a setup wizard opens, probes the endpoint to
# check the key works, and writes .env for you. ctrl+k reopens it later.
#
# Edit by hand when there is no terminal: a container, a scheduled task, CI.
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# The harness forwards these into the container by NAME, never by value, so a key
# never appears in a command line, a log or a trace.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- which provider
# openai   = any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (the default path)
# anthropic = Anthropic's own SDK
AGENT_PROVIDER=openai

# ------------------------------------------------------- OpenAI-compatible path
# One key variable for every such provider. Checked in order:
#   AGENT_API_KEY -> OPENAI_API_KEY -> NVIDIA_API_KEY
AGENT_API_KEY=

# Point these at whichever provider you want. The egress allowlist is DERIVED from
# the base URL, so the proxy follows the endpoint automatically - there is no
# firewall rule to remember to update.
#
#   NVIDIA NIM   https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1   nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b
#   OpenAI       https://api.openai.com/v1             gpt-4o
#   OpenRouter   https://openrouter.ai/api/v1          anthropic/claude-sonnet-4
#   Groq         https://api.groq.com/openai/v1        llama-3.3-70b-versatile
#   Ollama       http://localhost:11434/v1             agent-qwen25:7b
#                (inside the eval container it is http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1)
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1
OPENAI_MODEL=nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Anthropic
# Used when AGENT_PROVIDER=anthropic
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- BEFORE YOU RUN
# Check the endpoint can actually hold a tool-calling loop:
#
#   python eval/harness.py --check-provider
#
# This matters more than it looks. Tool calls are NEVER parsed out of free text,
# so a model that will not emit a well-formed call has no fallback - it just
# fails, mid-run, after spending quota. One open-weight model tried here leaked
# raw <tool_call> markup as message text and the run ended there.
#
# CHANGING THE MODEL INVALIDATES EVERY EXISTING BASELINE. It is a different
# measurement, not a tuning delta - the dev score once moved 4/15 -> 14/15 on a
# model swap alone, with no code change. Every trace row records provider and
# model so the two can never be confused.

# NIM_BASE_URL / NIM_MODEL still work as fallbacks for older .env files.
